    "topic": "Why pencils are yellow",
    "summary": "A large graphite deposit was found at Borrowdale, England, around 1565; it was thought to be a form of lead and called plumbago, which is why pencil cores are still called lead. According to Henry Petroski, the yellow-pencil tradition began in 1890 with L. & C. Hardtmuth's Koh-I-Noor brand. The colour may have been inspired by the Austro-Hungarian flag, and was suggestive of the Orient at a time when the best-quality graphite came from Siberia. Other companies copied it. Most pencils made in the US are painted yellow.",
    "angle": "A luxury brand's paint job became the default.",
